{"ts": 1789712475.837856, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted features 5, 14 and 31, all short, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.05 \u00b1 0.35. Its in-sample Sharpe was 0.711, which does not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.021, and I think that number is almost entirely selection.\n\n- **Single features:** I ran all 40. In-sample Sharpes ranged from \u22120.43 to +0.39. With 5,000 days (about 20 years), the standard error on a Sharpe is about 0.22, so the largest |z| is about 1.9. The expected maximum across 40 pure-noise features is about 2.2, so nothing stands out.\n- **Combinations:** Three-feature mixes of the largest-magnitude features (5, 14, 19 and 31) all gave 0.68\u20130.71. That is what three independent noise features at about 0.4 each would produce (0.4 \u00d7 3 / \u221a3 \u2248 0.7), so it is not evidence of real signal.\n- **Search:** `status` showed the best single feature at 0.393 against the 1.021 bar; I did not re-check after the combinations. I stopped after 45 evaluations rather than keep searching until something cleared it, because that would only add selection bias.\n\nThe mean of 0.05 reflects that the evidence fits zero true edge. The 0.35 standard deviation covers estimation noise in an out-of-sample window of unknown length.\n\nThe Gmail and Google Calendar connectors are not authorized and would need enabling in your claude.ai connector settings. They were not needed for this task."}
